{
  "id": "find-minimum-in-rotated-sorted-array",
  "questionId": "find-minimum-in-rotated-sorted-array",
  "slug": "find-minimum-in-rotated-sorted-array",
  "title": "Find Minimum in Rotated Sorted Array",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Binary search",
  "patterns": [
    "binary search"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "blind-75",
    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "the minimum sits exactly at the point where the array 'wraps' - everything before it is bigger than everything after it",
    "compare nums[mid] to nums[right]: if nums[mid] > nums[right], the minimum must be somewhere to the right of mid; otherwise it's at mid or to the left",
    "unlike a normal binary search, when nums[mid] <= nums[right] you can't rule out mid itself being the answer, so right should move to mid, not mid - 1",
    "keep narrowing until left and right meet - that position holds the minimum"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "comparing nums[mid] to nums[left] instead of nums[right], which doesn't reliably tell you which side the minimum is on",
    "setting right = mid - 1 when nums[mid] <= nums[right], accidentally skipping over the minimum itself",
    "assuming the array is always rotated and mishandling the already-sorted case"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "An array of distinct integers, originally sorted in ascending order, has\nbeen rotated an unknown number of positions to the right.\n\n**Return the minimum element in the array.**\n\nYou must do this faster than a plain linear scan.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= nums.length <= 5000",
      "-5000 <= nums[i] <= 5000",
      "all integers in nums are unique",
      "nums is sorted in ascending order and rotated between 1 and nums.length times"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "array is not rotated at all",
      "array has a single element",
      "the minimum is at the very last index"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
